# modules/sast/rules/javascript.rules - docs/DESIGN.md §6.3 "javascript/ts"
# seed catalog: eval, child_process.exec, dangerouslySetInnerHTML,
# document.write, template-literal SQL, require on user input, disabled TLS
# (rejectUnauthorized:false), prototype-pollution patterns (recursive merge /
# __proto__ assignment).
#
# Two catalog items are deliberately NOT re-implemented here, because the
# §13 step 3a engine ticket already shipped them as cross-language rules that
# match the exact JS syntax the catalog names:
#   - "disabled TLS (rejectUnauthorized:false)" is SAST-CRY-TLS_VERIFY_DISABLED-01
#     in modules/sast/rules/crypto.rules, whose pattern already alternates in
#     `rejectUnauthorized[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*false` alongside the
#     Python/Go spellings of the same control.
#   - "child_process.exec" is already one of the alternatives in
#     SAST-INJ-OS_COMMAND-01's pattern in modules/sast/rules/injection.rules
#     (that file is explicitly "language-agnostic sinks").
# Re-declaring either here under a new SAST-JS-* id would fire two check ids
# on the same line for the same root cause, which is strictly worse than one
# accurate finding: it inflates counts without adding coverage. See the
# hand-off comment on this ticket for the same note.
#
# Prototype pollution is split into two ids, following injection.rules'
# precedent of one id per distinct code shape rather than one id per catalog
# phrase: direct assignment to `__proto__` (SAST-JS-PROTO_ASSIGN-01), and the
# unguarded recursive-merge loop shape that is how attacker-controlled keys
# usually reach that assignment (SAST-JS-UNSAFE_MERGE-01).  UNSAFE_MERGE-01
# deliberately carries no `context-deny`: docs/FOUNDATION.md finding F4 says a
# deny window wider than 0 risks a false negative, and a guard clause for a
# recursive merge is never on the same line as the assignment it protects, so
# a window:0 deny could never see it - the only F4-safe choice here is not to
# attempt suppression at all, and let a genuinely different, non-matching
# shape (Object.assign / object-spread) be the safe equivalent instead.

id: SAST-JS-EVAL-01
title: Use of eval() to execute dynamic code
severity: critical
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-95
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: \beval\s*\(
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Do not pass request-derived or otherwise dynamic data to eval().
  Parse literals with JSON.parse(), or dispatch through an explicit allowlist of
  permitted operations instead of evaluating a string as code.
references: CWE-95

id: SAST-JS-DANGEROUSLY_SET_INNER_HTML-01
title: React dangerouslySetInnerHTML used to render markup
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: dangerouslySetInnerHTML
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
tags: static
remediation: Render content as text (React escapes it by default) instead of raw HTML.
  If HTML rendering is genuinely required, sanitize it through a vetted library such as
  DOMPurify immediately before it reaches dangerouslySetInnerHTML, every time it is used.
references: CWE-79

id: SAST-JS-DOCUMENT_WRITE-01
title: document.write() used to inject markup into the page
severity: medium
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-79
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: document\.write\s*\(
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
tags: static
remediation: Build the DOM with document.createElement()/textContent, or set
  element.textContent directly, rather than injecting a markup string with
  document.write(); it parses its argument as HTML with no escaping.
references: CWE-79

id: SAST-JS-TEMPLATE_LITERAL_SQL-01
title: SQL statement built from a JavaScript template literal
severity: critical
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-89
owasp: A03:2021
pattern: `[^`]*(SELECT|INSERT|UPDATE|DELETE)[^`]*\$\{
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
tags: static
tags: quick
remediation: Use a parameterised query (a placeholder plus a bound-values array) or an
  ORM/query builder that never interpolates request-derived data into SQL text; a
  template-literal `${}` interpolation inside a SQL string is string concatenation with
  extra syntax.
references: CWE-89

id: SAST-JS-DYNAMIC_REQUIRE-01
title: require() called with a non-literal module specifier
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-829
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: require\s*\(\s*[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$.]*\s*\)
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
tags: static
remediation: Pass require() a fixed string literal. If the module to load must vary at
  runtime, resolve it against an explicit allowlist of known module names first, so
  request-derived data can never select an arbitrary file to load.
references: CWE-829

id: SAST-JS-PROTO_ASSIGN-01
title: Direct assignment to __proto__
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-1321
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: (\.__proto__[[:space:]]*=|\[["']__proto__["']\][[:space:]]*=)
dialect: ere
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
tags: static
remediation: Never assign to __proto__ directly. Use Object.create(null) or a Map for a
  dictionary of attacker-influenced keys, so there is no prototype chain to pollute.
references: CWE-1321

id: SAST-JS-UNSAFE_MERGE-01
title: Recursive merge assigns an attacker-controlled key without a prototype guard
severity: high
confidence: medium
cwe: CWE-1321
owasp: A08:2021
pattern: \[[[:space:]]*key[[:space:]]*\][[:space:]]*=[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$]*\[[[:space:]]*key[[:space:]]*\]
dialect: ere
context-require: for[[:space:]]*\(
context-window: 2
files: *.js
files: *.jsx
files: *.ts
files: *.tsx
files: *.mjs
files: *.cjs
tags: static
remediation: Reject or skip "__proto__", "constructor" and "prototype" keys before
  assigning into the target of a recursive merge, or use Object.assign()/object-spread
  (which never copy onto the prototype) instead of a hand-written key-by-key loop.
references: CWE-1321
